17592186179137 #edb/ref 1080 "images/16/16d54b44282cb402b73ba6b67653a427b6de315d641f2d70f73f40b2acad95e2.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179137 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179137 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "583cfdf1-3d37-5e91-88a5-afcac53f5770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179138 #edb/ref 1080 "images/c5/c575c9898d27a6bf612d89655fcb78328a25333088588de9a4c8fe54944c26aa.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179138 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179138 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5ffbf08b-ad5b-5607-986c-6c1b54c934e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179139 #edb/ref 1080 "images/dd/dd73c2030aecbe3360a77e3ec75b2cb2f0785b2d4345cd1d9cb5c6d983855207.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179139 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179139 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6646d246-6302-5716-9049-52d5a2148be8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179511 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179511 #edb/ref 1071 "Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x} - 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c18caa2-840d-5911-ade5-63fd59752b64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179511 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}{\\color{red}\\,-\\,2}\n$$\n\nwe need to translate the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n*down* by $\\color{red}2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nis translated down by $2$ units, the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ is also translated down by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new horizontal asymptote is $y=-2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/b1/b157913f6fca24190330cdfa5c795e069eff15de8a2079d9e29e1d6e41b95cb4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179512 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179512 #edb/ref 1071 "Sketch the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ab32d98-1888-5ef0-95c1-81233cb7f449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179512 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x \\color{red} + 2}\n$$\n\nwe need to translate the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nto the *left* by $\\color{red}2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nis translated to the left by $2$ units, the vertical asymptote $x = 0$ is also translated to the left by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new vertical asymptote is $x=-2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/e8/e8bff71235e67d8ad4046e94b5fe2682543674877a253ae6d4fe431504dbde3c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179513 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179513 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-1}+2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5aee1873-5503-54ab-bf91-71a0abaf91a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179513 #edb/ref 1074 "The given function can be obtained by translating\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nboth horizontally and vertically.\n\nWe have two translations:\n\n- the $\\color{red}-1$ in the denominator means a horizontal translation of $1$ unit to the *right*, and\n- the $\\color{blue}+2$ outside the fraction means a vertical translation of $2$ units *up*.\n\nSo, the new graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fe/fea5a705657b22621f3eb5ffe40cfcd59aef11036674d47e6d1f72e301d531f3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179656 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179656 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179656 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179656 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179656 #edb/ref 1039 "Graphing Vertical Translations of Reciprocal Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179656 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179656 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7e14129e-c8c9-5c9b-8ad4-feb9d618dd6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179657 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179657 #edb/ref 1039 "Graphing Horizontal Translations of Reciprocal Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179657 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179657 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7bd87ed4-1b63-516e-9945-5de0c15e0c90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179658 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179658 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179658 #edb/ref 1039 "Combining Horizontal and Vertical Translations of Reciprocal Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179658 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179658 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "be4ff1c6-496e-5b3d-886f-5b6d21719469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1045 "Graph Transformations of Reciprocal Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179883 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5e5f11fe-e348-53cd-b6dc-ce7bd4a7602c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180104 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y = \\frac{1}{x + 4} + 2$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56d22403-432e-50ae-b57b-c4360be65083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180265 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186179134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180265 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180265 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y =-\\frac{1}{x - 2}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180265 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "039856a8-56a4-589f-b044-97bef589e14d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180364 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180364 #edb/ref 1061 19595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180364 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6fc045ed-1f66-5059-9322-147635824965" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180364 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180365 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180365 #edb/ref 1061 19597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180365 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb8634ab-c293-5d46-b057-a1e2064c7743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180365 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180366 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180366 #edb/ref 1061 19599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180366 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b64d3fe7-bf20-54ae-8ec5-3e3537b62a40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180366 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180367 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180367 #edb/ref 1061 19611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180367 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1886e869-3dac-5d58-87a0-374f861e3930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180367 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180368 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180368 #edb/ref 1061 19612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180368 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e213f8b6-ed0b-5850-bade-ae0621dbb5e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180368 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180369 #edb/ref 1061 19613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180369 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8c2a567-f4fc-54d4-9695-14458fe899fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180369 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180370 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180370 #edb/ref 1061 19615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180370 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb1b780c-22b7-56b9-98ce-88af12e62b74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180370 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180670 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}+k\n$$\n\nis a vertical translation of the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nby $k$ units up.\n\nFor example, to plot the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x} \\, {\\color{blue} \\, +\\, 2}\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve *up* by $\\color{blue}2$ units:\n\n![](images/2a/2aa8f50f8a40719e237c0e3d3706a3e161bfe1b107f6639c1df1a9aa8a956cbf.png)\n\nSimilarly, to plot the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x} \\, {\\color{red} - \\, 3}\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve *down* by $\\color{red}3$ units:\n\n![](images/45/45b81796c0140f9963afc26d13af1df9c9ac3f41de430f0dfee1192ab33bf42d.png)\n\nWith vertical translations of the reciprocal function, the vertical asymptote doesn't change, but the horizontal asymptote is translated by the same number of units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180670 #edb/ref 1065 3461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180670 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8393cefa-8bed-55ae-8c13-24021fc39b02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180670 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180671 #edb/ref 1064 "Because we know how to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwe can also plot horizontal translations of this graph. In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x-h}\n$$\n\nis a horizontal translation of the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nby $h$ units *right*.\n\nFor example, to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x {\\color{blue} \\,-\\, 1}}\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve horizontally by $\\color{blue}1$ unit *right*:\n\n![](images/d4/d4778ef277b11ab33eb01a650ad07c4fd2e3902f303dd2fb15ed81a4b54649a0.png)\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x+h}\n$$\n\nis a horizontal translation of the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nby $h$ units *left*.\n\nFor example, to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x{\\color{red} \\, + \\, 3}}\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve by $\\color{red}3$ units *left*:\n\n![](images/87/87bbb7c00e10db814c9fc67786940cf5891d96496a2e85ed7ee2023bb1af8e3d.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Horizontal translations are always counter-intuitive. The translation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x-h}\n$$\n\nis to the right and the translation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x+h}\n$$\n\nis to the left, even though it seems like it should be the other way around.\n\nWith horizontal translations of the reciprocal function, the horizontal asymptote doesn't change, but the vertical asymptote is translated by the same number of units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180671 #edb/ref 1065 3463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180671 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02a5b0c7-2b66-5387-a512-fc34f2371da0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180671 #edb/ref 1067 "Horizontal Translations of a Reciprocal Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180672 #edb/ref 1064 "We can combine horizontal and vertical translations of reciprocal functions.\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x-h} + k\n$$\n\nis obtained from the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nby translating it\n\n- horizontally by $h$ units to the right, and\n- vertically by $k$ units up.\n\nThe same is true regarding translations of the negative reciprocal function. In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{x-h} + k\n$$\n\nis obtained from the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nby translating it\n\n- horizontally by $h$ units to the right, and\n- vertically by $k$ units up." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180672 #edb/ref 1065 3465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180672 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a418bb7f-5146-523f-b21c-d3b1d2d1aa3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180672 #edb/ref 1067 "Combining Horizontal and Vertical Translations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180673 #edb/ref 1064 "We also know the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nso we can plot vertical translations of this function as well.\n\nFor example, to plot the function\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x} \\, {\\color{blue} \\, +\\, 1.5}\n$$\n\nwe translate the original curve *up* by $\\color{blue}1.5$ units:\n\n![](images/01/0189d3fe1630049f5b4c7b1c13b134cc77454008e51450db5bf8d85b2050eeb8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180673 #edb/ref 1065 3469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180673 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cd85bb48-e6a6-5094-a85a-9f2fc9f0ac95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180673 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Translations of a Negative Reciprocal Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
